---
<Layout title={title} description={description}>

<header>
  <h1>Understanding Focal Length — How 24mm, 50mm and 85mm Change Your Story</h1>
  <p class="hero-subtitle">Focal length isn't just "how zoomed in" a shot is — it's a perspective decision that changes how connected or distant your viewer feels to the subject. <strong>24mm tells the story of a place, 50mm feels like the way your own eyes see, and 85mm isolates a subject to make a moment feel emotionally private.</strong></p>
</header>

<article class="wrap">

  <div class="callout"><b>Quick take:</b> Wide lenses (24mm) sell environment and context — where the story is happening. Normal lenses (50mm) match natural human perspective and feel honest and unremarkable, which is exactly why they work so well for dialogue. Telephoto lenses (85mm and beyond) compress space and isolate a subject from a busy background, which reads as emotional focus. Same subject, same scene — three completely different feelings, purely from where you stand and what focal length you choose.</div>

  <h2>Focal Length Is a Storytelling Decision, Not Just a Zoom Level</h2>
  <p>Focal length, measured in millimetres, determines a lens's angle of view — how much of the scene fits inside the frame from a given distance. A small number like 24mm captures a wide angle of view; a large number like 85mm captures a narrow one. That much is basic optics, and most beginners stop there, treating focal length as nothing more than a way to fit more or less into the shot.</p>
  <p>But the more useful way to think about focal length on a film set is as a perspective tool. It changes how compressed the background looks relative to your subject, how much separation you get between subject and background, and — because of how the human eye interprets that compression — how emotionally close or distant a shot <em>feels</em> to an audience, independent of what's actually happening in the scene. A director blocking a scene in 24mm is making a very different emotional statement than the same blocking shot in 85mm, even if the subject's position in frame is identical.</p>

  <h2>Visual Comparison — Field of View Across Focal Lengths</h2>
