    <rect x="0" y="0" width="700" height="220" fill="none"></rect>
    <g>
      <polygon points="60,200 10,20 110,20" fill="#0f8b8d22" stroke="#0f8b8d" stroke-width="2"></polygon>
      <text x="60" y="215" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#0f8b8d" font-weight="700">24mm — Wide</text>
    </g>
    <g>
      <polygon points="350,200 320,20 380,20" fill="#0f8b8d33" stroke="#0f8b8d" stroke-width="2"></polygon>
      <text x="350" y="215" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#0f8b8d" font-weight="700">50mm — Normal</text>
    </g>
    <g>
      <polygon points="640,200 625,20 655,20" fill="#0f8b8d44" stroke="#0f8b8d" stroke-width="2"></polygon>
      <text x="640" y="215" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#0f8b8d" font-weight="700">85mm — Tele</text>
    </g>
  </svg>
  <p style="text-align:center; font-size:0.9rem; color:var(--ink-soft);">The field of view narrows as focal length increases — same subject distance, dramatically different framing and background relationship.</p>

  <h2>24mm — The Environmental Storyteller</h2>
  <p>24mm is a wide-angle lens that pulls a large amount of environment into frame. It's the focal length you reach for when the story needs the audience to understand <strong>where</strong> something is happening — establishing shots, wide interiors, landscapes, or any moment where the subject needs to feel connected to, or small within, their surroundings.</p>
  <p>Shot close to a face, 24mm produces obvious distortion — the nose stretches forward, ears recede, and features that should sit flat on a face start to bulge. That's why 24mm is a poor choice for tight portraits. It earns its keep when the subject occupies a modest part of the frame and the environment is doing real narrative work — a character dwarfed by a factory floor, a lone figure against a landscape, a room that needs to feel oppressive or expansive.</p>
  <div class="callout"><b>Use case:</b> Establishing shots, wide interiors, landscapes, and any scene where the environment itself is part of the story.</div>

  <h2>35mm — The Storytelling Middle Ground</h2>
  <p>35mm sits between the environmental pull of 24mm and the neutrality of 50mm, and that's exactly its appeal. It's wide enough to keep a sense of place in frame — useful for walk-and-talk scenes, two-shots, and documentary coverage where you need context and a subject in the same shot — while showing noticeably less distortion than 24mm when a face does drift toward the edge of frame.</p>
  <p>Many run-and-gun documentary shooters treat 35mm as a default focal length precisely because it's forgiving: close enough to intimate for a conversation, wide enough to catch a reaction from a second person stepping into frame without a lens change.</p>
  <div class="callout"><b>Use case:</b> Walk-and-talk scenes, wide two-shots, documentary coverage, environmental portraits with a bit more context than 50mm allows.</div>

  <h2>50mm — The Neutral, Honest Storyteller</h2>
  <p>50mm earns the nickname "nifty fifty" because its angle of view is close to how the human eye perceives a scene — not exaggerated wide, not compressed tight. That neutrality is precisely why 50mm reads as honest on screen. There's no perspective distortion pulling attention to itself, so the audience's focus stays entirely on performance and content rather than on the lens.</p>
  <p>For interviews, walk-and-talk coverage, and general B-roll, 50mm is the most reliably versatile focal length on set, because distortion is minimal and compression is moderate rather than extreme. That neutrality is exactly why our <a href="/best-beginner-lens-kit-cinematic-video">beginner lens kit guide</a> treats 50mm as the first prime lens worth owning.</p>
  <div class="callout"><b>Use case:</b> Interviews, general B-roll, documentary-style coverage, and as an all-purpose everyday lens.</div>

  <h2>85mm — The Emotional Isolator</h2>
  <p>85mm is a short telephoto, and its defining trait is strong subject-background separation. Because of the way telephoto compression works, background elements appear larger and closer to the subject than they actually are, producing a "squeezed" look that flattens depth and pulls the eye straight to the subject's face. Combined with a wide aperture, that compression is also what produces the smooth, creamy <a href="/depth-of-field-explained-bokeh-guide">background blur</a> that reads as high-end and cinematic.</p>
  <p>85mm is treated as an industry-standard portrait focal length because it renders facial features with almost no distortion — no exaggerated nose, no flattened ears, just an accurate, flattering representation of the face. That makes it the natural choice for emotional close-ups, tight interview reactions, and dramatic character moments where you want the audience locked onto a single face with the rest of the world falling away behind it. If you want to understand exactly why that happens optically, our <a href="/focal-length-perspective-facial-distortion-portraits">guide to focal length and facial distortion</a> breaks down the physics in more depth.</p>
  <div class="callout"><b>Use case:</b> Emotional close-ups, portrait-style interviews, dramatic reaction shots, and isolating a subject from a busy or distracting background.</div>

  <h2>135mm — The Extreme Compression Tool</h2>
  <p>Push past 85mm into 135mm territory and the effects only intensify. Background compression becomes dramatic — distant elements appear to sit right behind the subject's shoulder — and background separation becomes almost total, since even a modest aperture will melt a background into an abstract wash of colour and light. Working distance also increases substantially: at 135mm you're often shooting from several metres away, which is either a major advantage (an unaware or camera-shy subject barely notices they're being filmed) or a real limitation (you need space behind the camera that a small interior location simply doesn't have).</p>
  <p>135mm rarely appears as an everyday working lens on smaller productions, but it's a deliberate choice for extreme close-up emotional beats, long-lens documentary coverage where you can't get physically close to the subject, or any shot that wants to feel voyeuristic and distant despite showing a tight, intimate frame.</p>
  <div class="callout"><b>Use case:</b> Extreme emotional close-ups, long-lens documentary work, distant candid coverage, and shots that need maximum background compression.</div>

  <h2>Focal Length Quick-Reference Table</h2>
  <table>
